  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "0.12.0 — BREAKING on the 0.x line (minor bump is the 0.x breaking signal).\n\nBREAKING: `--json` and `--text` are now scoped to the commands that HAVE those projections\n(.github#1523). They used to be global, and on the verbs with no machine projection they were\naccepted and IGNORED — same prose, exit 0. They now REFUSE with exit 1 and a message naming the\nverb. Measured against this build's own `command-contract`, over all 40 verbs:\n\n  * `--json` is refused on 20 of 40: add, bootstrap, child, done, field-id, flush, followup,\n    heartbeat, item-id, landable, next, option-id, overlap, reap, release, room open, say,\n    set-field, verify-paths, whoami.\n  * `--text` is refused on 4 of 40: board, command-contract, issues, scan — the verbs that emit a\n    machine document and have no prose form to ask for.\n\nWHO THIS BREAKS: any script that appended `--json` for uniformity to a verb that never emitted JSON.\nOn 0.11.0 the PARSER accepted it on all twenty and handed control to the verb; on 0.12.0 the parser\nrefuses it before the verb runs. Observed side by side, 0.11.0 from nuget.org against this build:\n\n  $ fsgg-coord-engine whoami --json     # 0.11.0: prints the human text, exit 0\n                                        # 0.12.0: \"--json is not a flag of `whoami`\", exit 1\n\n`flush --json` and `reap --json` behave the same way. The other seventeen also reached their verb on\n0.11.0 and are stopped at the parser here; their exit code on 0.11.0 then depended on the verb's own\narguments and board access, so treat the parse-level acceptance as the thing that changed.\n\nTHE REMEDY IS TO DROP THE FLAG. Every BARE form is unchanged: all forty verbs were run bare against\n0.11.0 and against this build, and the only outputs that differ are `command-contract` (below) and\nthe `--help` text, which #1535 rewrote to say that `next` takes the chore lock.\n\nALSO CHANGED, for tooling that parses it: `command-contract` keeps schema id\n`fsgg.coord.commands/1` (the addition is additive) but its content moves twice — every row gains a\n`writes` key (.github#1534), and the twenty rows above no longer list `--json` among their flags.\nIt is the machine-readable answer to \"which verbs really take the flag\", and it is now correct.\n\nNOT BREAKING, and the reason to take this release: 0.11.0 corrupts touch-set declarations.\n`widen REF --paths TOKENS --json` swallowed `--json` INTO the declaration and exited 0 under a\nDISJOINT receipt (.github#1507), writing a junk token into the one line the scheduler reserves files\nby. Fixed here, along with `widen`/`set-paths` honouring `--json` (#1517), `reconcile --apply --json`\nemitting ONE document (#1524), `take --json`'s empty arm emitting a receipt instead of prose (#1525),\n`command-contract` carrying per-verb write-ness (#1534), and `next` being declared a board WRITE\nbecause it takes the chore lock (#1535).\n\nThe wire surface (src/FS.GG.Coord.Core/Protocol.fs) is UNCHANGED across all seven commits: the exit\ncodes and verb contract this repo's skills document are the ones this build enforces.",
